    "title": "Saint-Rémy, 1889–1890: setting and output",
    "text": "Vincent van Gogh entered the asylum of Saint-Paul-de-Mausole at Saint-Rémy-de-Provence in May 1889 and left a year later, in May 1890. Working in the grounds and in a room set aside as a studio, he produced a large body of paintings: wheat fields, olive groves, cypresses, irises and views of the enclosed garden. The surrounding hills, the wheat field seen from his window and the night sky recur across the group. His letters to his brother Theo are the main contemporary source for what he was painting and why, and they often name the canvases he had in progress."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "sn-02",
    "case_id": "starry_night",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Starry Night Over the Rhône (Arles, 1888)",
    "text": "Painted in Arles in September 1888, roughly nine months before the Saint-Rémy night sky, this canvas shows the Rhône at night with gaslight reflected in the water and the Great Bear above the horizon, with a couple in the foreground. Van Gogh described the night study to Theo in his letters. The handling is comparatively controlled: stars are distinct points of colour with soft halos, and the sky is built from short, even strokes. It is a useful point of comparison for how his treatment of the night sky changed between Arles and Saint-Rémy. The painting is now in the Musée d'Orsay, Paris."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "sn-03",
    "case_id": "starry_night",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Cypresses and the wheat field series (Saint-Rémy, 1889)",
    "text": "In June 1889 Van Gogh painted a group of cypress subjects at Saint-Rémy, including several versions of a wheat field with cypresses. In his letters he wrote that the trees preoccupied him and compared their line and proportion to an Egyptian obelisk. The trees are drawn as tall, dark, flame-like verticals, with thick strokes that follow the upward movement of the form, set against skies of curling, rhythmic brushwork. The same tree shape appears in the left foreground of other night and dusk compositions from the same months."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "sn-04",
    "case_id": "starry_night",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Road with Cypress and Star (Saint-Rémy, May 1890)",
    "text": "Painted in May 1890, shortly before Van Gogh left Saint-Rémy, this canvas places a single tall cypress at the centre of the composition, with a bright star and a crescent moon in a yellow-tinged sky, a road, travellers and a carriage. Outlines are strong and colour is applied in broad, directional strokes. The night sky is simplified compared with the swirling skies of the previous summer, so the picture is often used to show how his night imagery developed in the final months at the asylum. It is held by the Kröller-Müller Museum in Otterlo."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "sn-05",
    "case_id": "starry_night",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Van Gogh's brushwork in the Saint-Rémy paintings",
    "text": "In the Saint-Rémy paintings the direction, length and thickness of the stroke change with the motif. Skies and cypresses use long, curving or upward strokes with heavily loaded paint that stands in ridges (impasto). Fields and hills use shorter, more regular dabs, and outlines are often laid in with a darker, more fluid line. Strokes tend to follow the form or movement of what they describe, so the swirls in a sky are made by curved strokes rather than by blending. Sequences of parallel strokes in one colour and abrupt changes of direction at the edges of forms are typical of the period."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "sn-06",
    "case_id": "starry_night",
    "document_type": "secondary_description",
    "title": "The Starry Night (1889): subject and composition",
    "text": "The Starry Night is an oil painting on canvas of about 74 × 92 cm in landscape format, dated to mid-June 1889. A turbulent sky fills the upper two-thirds, with a large double spiral, a bright crescent moon at the upper right and eleven stars, each ringed by concentric halos. A tall dark cypress rises in the left foreground to the top edge. Below, a village with a church spire and small lit windows sits under rolling blue hills. The village is generally understood to be partly imagined, and the painting is commonly described as worked up from memory and imagination in the studio room, drawing on the view from his east-facing window, rather than painted on site at night. Blue and yellow dominate."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-01",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Utrecht Caravaggisti and candlelit meal scenes",
    "text": "Hendrick ter Brugghen, Gerard van Honthorst and Dirck van Baburen were painters from Utrecht who returned from Rome in the 1610s and 1620s having absorbed Caravaggio's dramatic lighting. Their pictures typically show half-length or three-quarter figures around a table, lit by a single artificial source such as a candle, with deep shadows and strong contrasts. Honthorst became known in Italy for his night scenes. Their meal and tavern subjects, with figures pressed close to the picture plane and expressive faces and gestures, spread through the Dutch Republic and influenced painters in other cities, including Leiden."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-02",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Leiden in the 1620s and 1630s: Lievens, Rembrandt and dramatic lighting",
    "text": "In the later 1620s Jan Lievens (1607–1674) and Rembrandt (1606–1669) worked closely in Leiden, sharing models and subjects and developing a manner of strong chiaroscuro, expressive faces and rough, varied surface texture. Biblical and domestic scenes with a dominant light source were central to this exchange. Rembrandt left for Amsterdam around 1631 to 1632, and Lievens left in the following years, but the Leiden taste for dramatic light and close-up figures remained influential. Painters born or trained in Leiden in these decades are often discussed in relation to this circle."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-03",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Leiden fijnschilders and candlelight interiors",
    "text": "Gerrit Dou (1613–1675), Rembrandt's first pupil in Leiden, developed a tradition of small, highly finished paintings, later called the Leiden fijnschilders ('fine painters'). Frans van Mieris the Elder (1635–1681) and Gabriel Metsu (born in Leiden in 1629, died 1667) worked in the same milieu of genre subjects. Godfried Schalcken (1643–1706), a pupil of Dou, specialised in candlelit interiors. The fijnschilder manner is smooth, minutely observed and small in scale, which contrasts with the broader handling and larger figures of the Utrecht and early Leiden tradition. It is a useful contrast when discussing brushwork claimed to be Leiden work."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-04",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Meals, grace and widowhood in Dutch painting",
    "text": "Prayer before a meal (grace) and the shared table are recurring subjects in seventeenth-century Dutch painting, appearing in religious, genre and portrait contexts. Elderly women shown alone or with a small household, often in modest, devout settings, are a common type, and charity toward widows and the poor was a familiar civic and religious theme in the Dutch Republic. Painters used a plain table, bread, a jug and a single light source to give such scenes moral weight, and viewers of the period would have read simple domestic objects as carrying meaning. The same compositional devices are found in works by many different painters."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-05",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Connoisseurship and expert opinion in attribution",
    "text": "Before scientific analysis became routine, attributions of old master paintings rested largely on connoisseurship: an expert's trained judgement of composition, brushwork, drawing of hands and ears, and overall quality, compared against securely attributed works. Figures such as Giovanni Morelli and Bernard Berenson formalised approaches of this kind in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Connoisseurship can identify stylistic consistency and quality and can be persuasive to dealers, museums and buyers. Its judgements are opinions and can differ between experts, so modern practice usually weighs them alongside documentary research and technical examination."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-06",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Cornelis van Leyden: career summary (game-world reference)",
    "text": "Cornelis van Leyden was a painter born in Leiden in the early seventeenth century and active from about 1630 into the 1660s. He worked in the dramatic-lighting manner shared by Leiden and Utrecht painters, favouring figures at a table under a single light source. About two dozen paintings are securely attributed to him. Most are modest in size, and a small number of large multi-figure canvases are counted among his most ambitious works. His life is thinly documented, and attributions of individual pictures have often rested on style and on comparison with the securely attributed group."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "ws-07",
    "case_id": "widows_supper",
    "document_type": "secondary_description",
    "title": "The Widow's Supper: subject and composition",
    "text": "The Widow's Supper is a large-format oil painting attributed to Cornelis van Leyden. An elderly woman sits at the centre of a plain table, her hands folded as if in grace. A young man sits at her right, and a serving girl stands at her left holding a jug. Bread and a simple meal are laid out on a white cloth. A single light source outside the picture, most plausibly a candle or lamp, falls on the faces and hands and leaves the background in deep shadow. The figures are close to the picture plane and are treated in broad, confident strokes."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "bh-01",
    "case_id": "blue_horses_dusk",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Der Blaue Reiter: origins and members",
    "text": "Der Blaue Reiter (The Blue Rider) was a loose association of artists formed in Munich in 1911 around Wassily Kandinsky and Franz Marc. It took its name from a planned almanac, published in 1912. The group organised two exhibitions in Munich, in December 1911 and February 1912. Associated artists included August Macke, Gabriele Münter, Alexej von Jawlensky and Paul Klee, and the composer Arnold Schoenberg contributed to the almanac. It was not a formal school with a shared programme. Its members favoured strong non-naturalistic colour, simplified forms and spiritual or symbolic subject matter, and the association ended with the outbreak of the First World War in 1914."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "bh-02",
    "case_id": "blue_horses_dusk",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "Franz Marc: animal imagery and colour symbolism",
    "text": "Franz Marc (1880–1916) made animals, and above all horses, the centre of his art. He assigned colours symbolic meanings: blue as masculine, spiritual and severe, yellow as feminine, gentle and sensual, and red as heavy, brutal matter. Works such as Blue Horse I (1911) use flattened, curving forms, saturated blue and strongly contrasted fields of colour so that the animal and the landscape echo each other's outlines. His animals are not observed studies but vehicles for a spiritual idea of nature. Marc was killed at Verdun in 1916."
  },
  {
    "doc_id": "bh-03",
    "case_id": "blue_horses_dusk",
    "document_type": "comparanda",
    "title": "August Macke and the Rhineland expressionists",
    "text": "August Macke (1887–1914) lived and worked in Bonn and was a central figure in the Rhineland avant-garde. He helped organise an exhibition of Rhenish expressionists in Bonn in 1913, and he was a close friend of Marc. His paintings favour clear, luminous colour and simplified figures in city, garden and landscape settings, and they are typically calmer and more decorative than the work of the Munich painters. Macke was killed in the first weeks of the First World War in September 1914, so many painters of the Rhineland circle developed their styles in the following decades without him."
  },
